  "concept_solutions": [
    {
      "block": 2,
      "independent_solution": "For a >= 0 and b >= 0, let r = sqrt(a)sqrt(b). Then r >= 0 and r^2 = ab. Uniqueness of the nonnegative number whose square is ab gives sqrt(ab) = r. This proves the product theorem and its sign restrictions. It does not license distributing sqrt over addition or subtraction, or taking individual real roots of negative factors."
    },
    {
      "block": 9,
      "independent_solution": "Apply the two-factor theorem repeatedly, or induct on the number of factors. A finite product of nonnegative factors is nonnegative, so sqrt(a1...an) = sqrt(a1)...sqrt(an). A zero factor makes both sides zero and remains allowed; a negative split factor is outside the real-root theorem."
    },
    {
      "block": 11,
      "independent_solution": "For a >= 0 and b > 0, sqrt(b) > 0. Set r = sqrt(a)/sqrt(b); then r >= 0 and r^2 = a/b. Uniqueness of the principal square root proves sqrt(a/b) = r, exactly following the definition-based proof requested in the paragraph. The strict condition b > 0 excludes a zero denominator and ensures both real roots used in the quotient exist."
    }
  ],
  "source_reviews": [
    {
      "source_number": "777",
      "subtask_ids": ["a", "b", "c", "d", "e", "f", "g", "h", "i"],
      "source_task": "Evaluate nine principal square roots of products of nonnegative perfect-square factors, including decimals and three-factor products.",
      "independent_solution": "Use the product theorem with nonnegative factors, taking the nonnegative root of each factor. (a) sqrt(100*49)=10*7=70. (b) sqrt(121*64)=11*8=88. (c) sqrt(81*400)=9*20=180. (d) sqrt(0.36*49)=0.6*7=4.2. (e) sqrt(144*0.25)=12*0.5=6. (f) sqrt(2.25*0.04)=1.5*0.2=0.3. (g) sqrt(9*64*25)=3*8*5=120. (h) sqrt(0.16*0.0025*900)=0.4*0.05*30=0.6; notably sqrt(0.0025)=0.05, not 0.5. (i) sqrt(1.21*0.09*0.01)=1.1*0.3*0.1=0.033. Each answer is nonnegative and its square equals the full original product."
    },
    {
      "source_number": "778",
      "subtask_ids": ["a", "b", "c", "d", "e", "f", "g", "h"],
      "source_task": "Evaluate fractional roots, first converting both mixed numerals to improper fractions.",
      "independent_solution": "All numerators are nonnegative and denominators positive, so apply the quotient theorem. (a) sqrt(16/25)=4/5. (b) sqrt(9/64)=3/8. (c) sqrt(64/49)=8/7. (d) sqrt(121/25)=11/5. (e) sqrt(144/169)=12/13. (f) sqrt(225/256)=15/16. (g) 1 9/16=25/16, hence sqrt(1 9/16)=5/4. (h) 2 7/81=169/81, hence sqrt(2 7/81)=13/9. The mixed-number conversion is essential: the integer part is inside the radical."
    },
    {
      "source_number": "779",
      "subtask_ids": ["a", "b", "c", "d", "e", "f"],
      "source_task": "Regroup products whose individual factors need not be perfect squares into perfect-square products.",
      "independent_solution": "(a) 810*40=81*400=32400, so sqrt=9*20=180. (b) 72*32=9*256=2304, so sqrt=3*16=48. (c) 75*48=25*144=3600, so sqrt=5*12=60. (d) 4.9*360=49*36=1764, so sqrt=7*6=42. (e) 2.5*14.4=36, so sqrt=6; equivalently (5/2)*(72/5)=36. (f) 90*6.4=576=36*16, so sqrt=6*4=24. Regrouping or exact multiplication, not independent rounding of irrational roots, supplies the exact values."
    },
    {
      "source_number": "780",
      "subtask_ids": ["a", "b"],
      "source_task": "Prove two strict inequalities; square-root product rules do not distribute over sums or differences.",
      "independent_solution": "(a) sqrt(3^2+4^2)=sqrt(9+16)=sqrt(25)=5, while 3+4=7; therefore 5<7 proves the requested inequality. Alternatively (3+4)^2=49>25 and both sides are nonnegative. (b) sqrt(10^2-6^2)=sqrt(100-36)=sqrt(64)=8, while 10-6=4; therefore 8>4. Equivalently (10-6)^2=16<64. These computations prove the stated inequalities and explicitly distinguish sqrt(u^2+v^2) from u+v and sqrt(u^2-v^2) from u-v."
    },
    {
      "source_number": "781",
      "subtask_ids": ["a", "b", "c", "d"],
      "source_task": "Evaluate differences of squares under principal square roots by factoring.",
      "independent_solution": "Use u^2-v^2=(u-v)(u+v). (a) (13-12)(13+12)=1*25=25, so the root is 5. (b) (313-312)(313+312)=1*625=625, so the root is 25. (c) (17-8)(17+8)=9*25=225, so the root is 15. (d) (117-108)(117+108)=9*225=2025, so the root is 45. The difference u-v alone is not the requested square root."
    },
    {
      "source_number": "782",
      "subtask_ids": ["a", "b", "c", "d"],
      "source_task": "Combine product and quotient square-root laws with decimals and mixed-number conversion.",
      "independent_solution": "(a) sqrt((25/81)*(16/49)*(196/9))=(5/9)*(4/7)*(14/3)=280/189=40/27. (b) sqrt(0.36*0.25*144)=0.6*0.5*12=3.6. (c) 3 1/16=49/16 and 2 14/25=64/25; their product root is (7/4)*(8/5)=14/5=2.8. (d) 5 1/16=81/16 and 2 34/81=196/81; the root is (9/4)*(14/9)=7/2=3.5. Every mixed numeral is converted before multiplication; all factors lie in the real nonnegative domain."
    },
    {
      "source_number": "783",
      "subtask_ids": ["a", "b", "c", "d"],
      "source_task": "Express each radical as a product of square roots while retaining real-domain restrictions.",
      "independent_solution": "(a) sqrt(15)=sqrt(3)*sqrt(5). (b) sqrt(21)=sqrt(3)*sqrt(7). (c) sqrt(7a)=sqrt(7)*sqrt(a), on its real domain a>=0. (d) sqrt(-3c)=sqrt(3)*sqrt(-c), on its real domain c<=0, because -3c=3*(-c) has two nonnegative factors there. Splitting (d) into sqrt(-3)*sqrt(c) is invalid over the reals even when the original radical exists. The decompositions are not unique, but these use meaningful nonnegative factors; boundary a=0 or c=0 is permitted."
    },
    {
      "source_number": "784",
      "subtask_ids": ["a", "b", "c", "d"],
      "source_task": "Express each radical as a quotient of two real radicals, with a strictly positive denominator radical.",
      "independent_solution": "(a) sqrt(2/7)=sqrt(2)/sqrt(7). (b) sqrt(3/10)=sqrt(3)/sqrt(10). (c) sqrt(5/a)=sqrt(5)/sqrt(a), with a>0: a=0 is undefined and a<0 gives a negative radicand. (d) sqrt(-b/3)=sqrt(-b)/sqrt(3), with b<=0. For (d), b=0 gives zero and is allowed; placing the negative sign under sqrt(3) instead would not be a real quotient. These equalities retain the original real domains."
    },
    {
      "source_number": "785",
      "subtask_ids": ["a", "b", "c", "d", "e", "f", "g", "h", "i"],
      "source_task": "Evaluate products of real square roots by combining radicands.",
      "independent_solution": "All radicands are positive, so sqrt(u)*sqrt(v)=sqrt(uv). (a) sqrt(2)*sqrt(8)=sqrt(16)=4. (b) sqrt(10)*sqrt(40)=sqrt(400)=20. (c) sqrt(12)*sqrt(3)=sqrt(36)=6. (d) sqrt(27)*sqrt(3)=sqrt(81)=9. (e) sqrt(21)*sqrt(21^3)=sqrt(21^4)=21^2=441. (f) sqrt(13)*sqrt(52)=sqrt(676)=26. (g) sqrt(63)*sqrt(7)=sqrt(441)=21. (h) sqrt(2/3)*sqrt(3/8)=sqrt(1/4)=1/2. (i) 1.2=6/5 and 3 1/3=10/3, so sqrt(1.2)*sqrt(3 1/3)=sqrt(4)=2."
    },
    {
      "source_number": "786",
      "subtask_ids": ["a", "b", "c", "d", "e", "f"],
      "source_task": "Evaluate quotients of positive radicals; preserve seed source ID 786 despite prose heading 736.",
      "independent_solution": "Apply sqrt(u)/sqrt(v)=sqrt(u/v), since each v>0. (a) sqrt(2)/sqrt(18)=sqrt(1/9)=1/3. (b) sqrt(23)/sqrt(2300)=sqrt(1/100)=1/10. (c) sqrt(999)/sqrt(111)=sqrt(9)=3. (d) sqrt(15)/sqrt(735)=sqrt(1/49)=1/7. (e) sqrt(52)/sqrt(117)=sqrt(4/9)=2/3, using 52=4*13 and 117=9*13. (f) sqrt(12500)/sqrt(500)=sqrt(25)=5. Values are attached to seed number 786, not to an inferred correction of its printed heading."
    },
    {
      "source_number": "787",
      "subtask_ids": ["a", "b", "c", "d", "e", "f", "g", "h"],
      "source_task": "Rationalize denominators and write rational coefficient times a natural-number radical; prose heading is 737.",
      "independent_solution": "Multiply numerator and denominator by the denominator radical, then reduce rational coefficients and extract square factors. (a) 5/sqrt(10)=5sqrt(10)/10=(1/2)sqrt(10). (b) 7/sqrt(5)=(7/5)sqrt(5). (c) -6/sqrt(2)=-6sqrt(2)/2=-3sqrt(2). (d) -12/sqrt(3)=-12sqrt(3)/3=-4sqrt(3). (e) 5/(2sqrt(3))=5sqrt(3)/6=(5/6)sqrt(3). (f) 21/(5sqrt(14))=21sqrt(14)/70=(3/10)sqrt(14). (g) -3sqrt(5)/sqrt(2)=-3sqrt(10)/2=-(3/2)sqrt(10). (h) -7sqrt(6)/sqrt(3)=-7sqrt(2). In all eight results the coefficient is rational and the remaining radicand is a positive natural number; all original minus signs remain."
    },
    {
      "source_number": "788",
      "subtask_ids": ["a", "b", "c", "d"],
      "source_task": "Evaluate products and quotients of even powers inside roots; prose heading is 738.",
      "independent_solution": "All bases are positive, so halve even exponents when taking their principal roots. (a) sqrt(2^6*3^4)=2^3*3^2=8*9=72. (b) sqrt(5^4*2^6)=5^2*2^3=25*8=200. (c) sqrt((3^2*5^6)/10^4)=(3*5^3)/10^2=375/100=15/4. (d) sqrt(7^2/(2^6*5^4))=7/(2^3*5^2)=7/(8*25)=7/200. Quotient denominators are positive, and neither an outer denominator nor an exponent is left unrooted."
    },
    {
      "source_number": "789",
      "subtask_ids": ["a", "b", "c", "d"],
      "source_task": "First express each integer radicand as a product of powers, then evaluate its root.",
      "independent_solution": "(a) 1225=5^2*7^2, hence sqrt(1225)=5*7=35. (b) 3969=3^4*7^2, hence sqrt(3969)=3^2*7=63. (c) 19600=2^4*5^2*7^2, hence sqrt(19600)=2^2*5*7=140. (d) 44100=2^2*3^2*5^2*7^2, hence sqrt(44100)=2*3*5*7=210. The power products are part of the requested task, not just ancillary final-value checks."
    },
    {
      "source_number": "790",
      "subtask_ids": ["a", "b", "c", "d", "e", "f"],
      "source_task": "Evaluate by scaling to a square-table-sized integer and following the demonstrated product/quotient format.",
      "independent_solution": "Independently verify the needed integer squares; no back-of-book table or answer source is opened. (a) 448900=4489*100, 4489=67^2, so sqrt(448900)=sqrt(4489)*sqrt(100)=67*10=670. (b) 115600=1156*100, 1156=34^2, so the root is 34*10=340. (c) 7840000=784*10000, 784=28^2, so the root is 28*100=2800. (d) 51.84=5184/100, 5184=72^2, so the root is 72/10=7.2. (e) 62.41=6241/100, 6241=79^2, so the root is 79/10=7.9. (f) 0.7744=7744/10000, 7744=88^2, so the root is 88/100=0.88. The unlettered 96100 and 26.01 computations in the seed are demonstrations of format, not extra answer slots or external answer authority."
    },
    {
      "source_number": "791",
      "subtask_ids": ["a", "b", "c", "d", "e", "f"],
      "source_task": "Scale supplied three-significant-digit square-root approximations using powers of ten.",
      "independent_solution": "Use exact identities to transport only the supplied precision: sqrt(2.35) approximately 1.53 and sqrt(23.5) approximately 4.85. (a) sqrt(235)=10sqrt(2.35), hence approximately 15.3. (b) sqrt(2350)=10sqrt(23.5), hence approximately 48.5. (c) sqrt(23500)=100sqrt(2.35), hence approximately 153. (d) sqrt(0.235)=sqrt(23.5)/10, hence approximately 0.485. (e) sqrt(0.0235)=sqrt(2.35)/10, hence approximately 0.153. (f) sqrt(0.00235)=sqrt(23.5)/100, hence approximately 0.0485. Every result has three significant digits. A factor 100 in the radicand becomes a factor 10 in the root; leading zeros are not significant and the approximate sign is essential."
    },
    {
      "source_number": "792",
      "subtask_ids": ["a", "b", "c", "d"],
      "source_task": "Produce monomials from square roots of squared variable expressions, using each stated sign condition.",
      "independent_solution": "Use sqrt(u^2)=|u|. (a) sqrt(9a^2)=3|a|=3a for a>=0, including a=0. (b) sqrt(25b^2)=5|b|=5b for b>0. (c) sqrt(0.36x^2)=0.6|x|=-0.6x for x<0. (d) sqrt(81y^6)=9|y^3|=-9y^3 for y<=0, because y^3<=0; y=0 is valid. The expressions with a minus sign in (c,d) still have nonnegative values on their given domains. Dropping the absolute value without the sign check would change those two answers."
    },
    {
      "source_number": "793",
      "subtask_ids": ["a", "b"],
      "source_task": "Express each radical as a fraction of monomials, accounting for denominator sign.",
      "independent_solution": "(a) sqrt(49/x^2)=7/sqrt(x^2)=7/|x|=7/x for x>0. (b) sqrt(144/y^2)=12/sqrt(y^2)=12/|y|=-12/y for y<0. Both denominators are nonzero by the givens, and the fractions are positive. In (b), 12/y alone is negative and cannot equal the principal root. The sign-adjusted denominator expression is necessary even though the original denominator is a square."
    },
    {
      "source_number": "794",
      "subtask_ids": ["a", "b"],
      "source_task": "Construct approximate cubic graphs and determine the sets of strict increase/decrease, without any source image.",
      "independent_solution": "(a) y=x^3 is defined on all real x, is odd and symmetric about the origin, and passes through (-1,-1), (0,0), (1,1); both domain and range are R. It runs from lower left to upper right with a horizontal tangent at the origin, which is not a maximum or minimum. For x1<x2, x2^3-x1^3=(x2-x1)(x2^2+x1*x2+x1^2)>0: the second factor=(x2+x1/2)^2+3*x1^2/4 and cannot be zero for two distinct numbers. Thus the increase set is R and there is no nontrivial decreasing interval. (b) y=-x^3 is the reflection in the x-axis, passes through (-1,1), (0,0), (1,-1), is odd, and runs from upper left to lower right. Negating the difference makes it strictly negative for x1<x2, so the decrease set is R and there is no nontrivial increasing interval. These are analytic graph specifications only; no drawing or image has been opened or approved."
    },
    {
      "source_number": "795",
      "subtask_ids": ["a", "b", "c", "d"],
      "source_task": "Construct quadratic graphs and give monotonicity intervals for two numerical and two sign-constrained coefficients.",
      "independent_solution": "For y=a*x^2 with a nonzero, the graph is even, symmetric about the y-axis, has vertex (0,0), and domain R. For x1<x2<=0, x2^2-x1^2=(x2-x1)(x2+x1)<0; for 0<=x1<x2, this difference is >0, so 0 can be included at both half-line endpoints. (a) a=1.73>0: upward parabola, minimum 0, range [0,infinity), anchors (-1,1.73),(0,0),(1,1.73); strictly decreasing on (-infinity,0] and increasing on [0,infinity). (b) a=-14.8<0: downward parabola, maximum 0, range (-infinity,0], anchors (-1,-14.8),(0,0),(1,-14.8); increasing on (-infinity,0] and decreasing on [0,infinity). (c) For arbitrary a>0, the upward orientation, minimum, range and intervals are exactly as in (a); anchors (-1,a),(0,0),(1,a) depend on a, so no single fixed width is specified. (d) For arbitrary a<0, the downward orientation, maximum, range and intervals are as in (b), with the same parameterized anchors. The open half-lines also satisfy monotonicity but are not maximal. No interval crossing 0 is monotone for any of these four parabolas; a=0 is excluded in (c,d). No image observations or approvals are claimed."
    },
    {
      "source_number": "796",
      "subtask_ids": ["a", "b"],
      "source_task": "Intersect two strict inequality solution sets and select all integer solutions.",
      "independent_solution": "(a) (5x-1)/3<2 gives 5x-1<6, hence x<7/5; 3x-0.8>0.2 gives 3x>1, hence x>1/3. Their intersection is (1/3,7/5); the only integer in it is 1, so the complete integer set is {1}. (b) 1>(3-x)/4 gives 4>3-x, hence x>-1; 0.6x-3<2 gives 0.6x<5, hence x<25/3. Their intersection is (-1,25/3), so the complete integer set is {0,1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8}. All endpoints are strict. Reversing the sign when isolating x in the first inequality of (b) is necessary; -1 is excluded and 8 is included."
    },
    {
      "source_number": "797",
      "subtask_ids": ["a", "b"],
      "source_task": "Recognize perfect-square trinomials, use absolute values, and substitute the given positive numbers.",
      "independent_solution": "(a) 1-10a+25a^2=(5a-1)^2, so the expression is |5a-1|-4a. At a=15.2, 5a-1=75>0, and the value is 75-60.8=14.2 (also a-1=14.2 on this branch). (b) 1+6x+9x^2=(3x+1)^2, so the expression is 4x-|3x+1|. At x=3.18, 3x+1=10.54>0 and 4x=12.72, giving 12.72-10.54=2.18 (also x-1=2.18 on this branch). The general identities retain absolute values: the branch a-1 requires a>=1/5, and the branch x-1 requires x>=-1/3. They are not unconditional simplifications."
    },
    {
      "source_number": "798",
      "subtask_ids": [],
      "unlettered_task": "Prove the universal strict bound for every real x>5.",
      "source_task": "Factor and simplify a rational expression, preserve its domain, and prove both strict inequalities for all x>5.",
      "independent_solution": "Let E=((x+5)/(x^2-5x)-x/(x^2-25))*((x^2-25)/5). For x>5, x, x-5 and x+5 are all nonzero, so every original denominator is defined and cancellation is legitimate. Factor x^2-5x=x(x-5) and x^2-25=(x-5)(x+5). Combining the bracket over x(x-5)(x+5) yields ((x+5)^2-x^2)/(x(x-5)(x+5))=(10x+25)/(x(x^2-25)). Multiplying by (x^2-25)/5 then gives E=(10x+25)/(5x)=2+5/x. Since x>5, 0<5/x<1, hence 2<E<3 for every allowed x, not just numerical samples. Equivalently E-2=5/x>0 and 3-E=(x-5)/x>0. Neither endpoint is attained: E approaches 3 as x approaches 5 from above and approaches 2 as x grows without bound. The proof must retain the original x>5 condition, not treat the cancelled expression as authorizing excluded inputs."
    }
  ]
}
